 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Power dissipation",
   "expr": "PTOT = rDS(on) × (IOUT(RMS))²",
   "where": {
    "PTOT": "total dissipation in the device",
    "rDS(on)": "combined resistance of the conducting high-side and low-side FETs",
    "IOUT(RMS)": "rms (or DC) current delivered to the load"
   },
   "note": "The FET resistance dominates the losses. rDS(on) rises as the die warms, so dissipation grows with temperature; how much the device can dissipate depends on ambient temperature and heatsinking.",
   "page": 20
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Four N-channel power FETs form one H-bridge between OUT1 and OUT2; a charge pump makes the gate drive and an oscillator, logic, per-FET overcurrent sensing and an overtemperature monitor sit alongside. Typical loads are one DC motor or a solenoid. The DRV8837 controls the bridge from IN1 and IN2, the DRV8838 from PH and EN, and both have an nSLEEP pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bridge control",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "On the DRV8837 each input steers its own output: IN1 high and IN2 low drives forward, the opposite drives reverse, both high brakes with both outputs low, and both low leaves the outputs high impedance (coast). On the DRV8838, EN low brakes, and with EN high the PH level picks forward (PH low) or reverse (PH high). nSLEEP low coasts either device whatever the inputs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Independent half-bridges",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "With a load from VM to each output and the controller signals crossed (C1 to IN2, C2 to IN1), each half of the DRV8837 runs one load with no extra parts. A high Cx drives its load. A low Cx puts that half in one of two braking modes. In braking mode 1 (both Cx low) the high- and low-side FETs are both off and the load current recirculates through the high-side body diode. In braking mode 2 (the other Cx high) the low-side FET stays off while the high-side FET conducts, so the current recirculates through it. With independent PWM on C1 and C2 each half mixes the two modes; the more time spent in mode 1, the more power is lost in the body diode and the slower the motor runs on average. Schottky diodes under 0.6 V forward drop across each motor cut that loss. Steady on/off control (no PWM) does not let the two modes interact, and the average speeds are unaffected."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sleep mode",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Pulling nSLEEP low powers down everything not needed and leaves the bridge FETs off (high impedance). Taking nSLEEP high wakes the part on its own within twake (30 µs max). Holding every input low during sleep keeps system power lowest."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supplies and inputs",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "The inputs may be driven within their recommended range whether or not VCC and VM are present; there is no leakage path from an input into a supply. Each input has a weak pulldown of about 100 kΩ to ground. VCC and VM may come up and go down in any order. With VCC removed the part draws very little from VM. VCC and VM can share one supply when it lies between 1.8 V and 7 V. VM has no undervoltage lockout: while VCC is above 1.8 V the logic stays active even with VM at 0 V, though the load cannot be driven properly at low VM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "VCC undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "When VCC drops below the UVLO threshold all bridge FETs turn off; they resume once VCC climbs back above it (1.7 V falling, 1.8 V rising, both maximum)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overcurrent protection",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Every FET, high side and low side, has an analog current limit that pulls its gate drive back. If the limit is active for longer than tDEG (1 µs typ), the whole bridge turns off, and it restarts by itself after tRETRY (1 ms typ). A short from an output to VM or GND, or between OUT1 and OUT2, trips it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "If the die gets too hot (TTSD, 150 °C min) the bridge turns off, and it restarts by itself once the die has cooled. Frequent trips mean the part is dissipating too much, is poorly cooled, or sits in an ambient that is too warm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Motor voltage",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "VM follows the motor's rating and the speed wanted: at a given PWM duty a higher VM turns a brushed DC motor faster and also makes the winding current change faster."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bulk capacitance",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Local bulk capacitance on VM, next to the small ceramic bypass, keeps the motor supply steady when the motor draws or returns current faster than the supply wiring's inductance allows. More is generally better, at the cost of size and price. How much is needed depends on the peak motor current, the supply's own capacitance and current capability, the wiring inductance, the acceptable ripple, the motor type and the braking method; system testing settles the value. Rate the bulk capacitor above the operating voltage, since the motor can push energy back into the supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "Bypass VM and VCC to GND with low-ESR 0.1 µF ceramics rated for their voltages, as close to the pins as possible, with a wide trace or a ground plane back to the GND pin; each capacitor sits beside its own pin, VM at pin 1 and VCC at pin 8. The exposed pad is soldered to a board pad that may carry two optional vias; vias under paste are best filled, plugged or tented."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Land pattern and stencil",
   "page": 28,
   "text": "Board land pattern (reference values, mm): eight pads 0.5 long by 0.25 wide at 0.5 pitch, the two rows 1.9 apart centre to centre; centre pad 0.9 by 1.6 with two optional 0.2 vias, 0.55 above and below its centre; corner radius 0.05. Non-solder-mask-defined pads (preferred) use a mask opening up to 0.07 larger than the metal all round; solder-mask-defined pads put the mask edge at least 0.07 inside the metal. The 0.125 mm stencil prints the centre pad as two 0.9 by 0.7 openings centred 0.45 either side of the pad centre, for 87 % paste coverage by area."
  }
